### Output (5/5 runs, both utls versions)

```
id, first_handshake_ok, first_did_resume, second_handshake_ok, second_did_resume
HelloGolang (control, no ApplyPreset)         first_ok=true  first_resume=false second_ok=true  second_resume=true
HelloChrome_120 (named preset, via ApplyPreset) first_ok=true  first_resume=false second_ok=true  second_resume=false
```

### Expected

Both `HelloGolang` and `HelloChrome_120` should resume on the second handshake — the session ticket is issued and cached identically in both cases (`Get`/`Put` both fire, confirmed by instrumenting the cache), and nothing else about the harness differs between the two runs except the `ClientHelloID` passed to `UClient`.

### Observed

`HelloGolang`, which never calls `ApplyPreset` (it builds its ClientHello via `makeClientHello` instead), resumes reliably. Every `ClientHelloID` we've tried that *does* go through `UConn.ApplyPreset` — including named mimicry presets like `HelloChrome_120` here, and in our own (more involved) usage also a `HelloCustom` with a hand-built `ClientHelloSpec` — never resumes, even though the handshake itself completes successfully and reports a session ticket was received.

### Additional context

We hit this in [ghostscrape](https://github.com/Makrorof/ghostscrape) (a browser-TLS-mimicry HTTP client), where `ApplyPreset`-based mimicry is the whole point, so falling back to `HelloGolang` isn't an option for us. We also can't currently move past the vendored pseudo-version to see if a newer *unreleased* commit fixes this, because [refraction-networking/uquic](https://github.com/refraction-networking/uquic) — which we also depend on for HTTP/3 fingerprinting — pins this exact utls pseudo-version and fails to compile against `v1.8.2` (utls removed `UQUICConn.StoreSession` between the two). Happy to provide more detail or test a patch against either dependency if that's useful.

